{
  "dom_id": "step-q130790",
  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "Given that $2x+6=5x$, what is the value of $x?$",
  "worked_solution": "We must bring all variables to one side of the equation and all constants to the other side. \n\nNotice that we have $2x$ on the left-hand side and $5x$ on the right-hand side. Since the variable on the right-hand side has a larger coefficient, let's bring all the variables to this side. \n\nThe first step is to remove the $2x$ from the left-hand side. We do this by subtracting $2x$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+6 & =5x \\\\ 2x+6-2x & =5x-2x \\\\ 6 & =3x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide both sides of the equation by $3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6 & =3x \\\\ \\frac{6}{3} & =\\frac{3x}{3} \\\\ 2 & =\\frac{\\cancel{3}x}{\\cancel{3}} \\\\ 2 & =x \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
